  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#RelativePermittivity">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The `relative permittivity` of a material under given conditions reflects the extent to which it concentrates electrostatic lines of flux. In technical terms, it is the ratio of the amount of electrical energy stored in a material by an applied voltage, relative to that stored in a vacuum. Likewise, it is also the ratio of the capacitance of a capacitor using that material as a dielectric, compared to a similar capacitor that has a vacuum as its dielectric. Relative permittivity is a dimensionless number that is in general complex. The imaginary portion of the permittivity corresponds to a phase shift of the polarization P relative to E and leads to the attenuation of electromagnetic waves passing through the medium.
ε_r(w) = (ε(w))/(ε_O) where ε_r(w) is the complex frequency-dependent absolute permittivity of the material, and ε_O is the vacuum permittivity.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/unclassifiedunits#"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/PERMITTIVITY_REL</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb3143"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb3142"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c2f5ee66_579c_44c6_a2e9_fa2eaa9fa4da"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_15344998_4490_4b40_b242_c3d92e5cdc27"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relative_static_permittivity?oldid=334224492</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://www.ncert.nic.in/html/learning_basket/electricity/electricity/charges%20&amp;%20fields/absolute_permittivity.htm</rdfs:seeAlso>
    <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">[eps_0]</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Relative Permittivity</skos:altLabel>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">The `relative permittivity` of a material under given conditions reflects the extent to which it concentrates electrostatic lines of flux. In technical terms, it is the ratio of the amount of electrical energy stored in a material by an applied voltage, relative to that stored in a vacuum. Likewise, it is also the ratio of the capacitance of a capacitor using that material as a dielectric, compared to a similar capacitor that has a vacuum as its dielectric. Relative permittivity is a dimensionless number that is in general complex. The imaginary portion of the permittivity corresponds to a phase shift of the polarization P relative to E and leads to the attenuation of electromagnetic waves passing through the medium.
ε_r(w) = (ε(w))/(ε_O) where ε_r(w) is the complex frequency-dependent absolute permittivity of the material, and ε_O is the vacuum permittivity.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">RelativePermittivity</skos:prefLabel>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">εᵣ</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">RelativePermittivity</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
