  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#PlanckEnergy">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">PlanckEnergy</skos:prefLabel>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Eᵨ</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planck_energy?oldid=493639955</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb2963"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb2962"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_15344998_4490_4b40_b242_c3d92e5cdc27"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_f6070071_d054_4b17_9d2d_f446f7147d0f"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PlanckEnergy</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/unclassifiedunits#"/>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">In physics, the unit of energy in the system of natural units known as Planck units is called the Planck energy, denoted by Eₚ. Eₚ is a derived, as opposed to basic, Planck unit. An equivalent definition is: Eₚ = ℏ / tₚ where tₚ is the Planck time. Also: Eₚ = mₚ c² where mₚ is the Planck mass.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Planck Energy</skos:altLabel>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/PlanckEnergy</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">In physics, the unit of energy in the system of natural units known as Planck units is called the Planck energy, denoted by Eₚ. Eₚ is a derived, as opposed to basic, Planck unit. An equivalent definition is: Eₚ = ℏ / tₚ where tₚ is the Planck time. Also: Eₚ = mₚ c² where mₚ is the Planck mass.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
