  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#Foot">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8246541a_f1f6_4d03_8bd7_fc6b76d17375"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1092"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1091"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_b3600e73_3e05_479d_9714_c041c3acf5cc"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Foot</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/specialunits#"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">A foot is a unit of length defined as being 0.3048 m exactly and used in the imperial system of units and United States customary units. It is subdivided into 12 inches. The foot is still officially used in Canada and still commonly used in the United Kingdom, although the latter has partially metricated its units of measurement.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/FT</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A foot is a unit of length defined as being 0.3048 m exactly and used in the imperial system of units and United States customary units. It is subdivided into 12 inches. The foot is still officially used in Canada and still commonly used in the United Kingdom, although the latter has partially metricated its units of measurement.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">[ft_i]</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ft</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Foot</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#Hour">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_1abe0ea7_2378_402f_b950_89b0795d2f45"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1304"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_9e5f464a_658c_4b34_b80d_a5f8d5ec811a"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1302"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0266ec8c_bd13_4c9c_be77_607498793ed3"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_02e894c3_b793_4197_b120_3442e08f58d1"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1303"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_6795a4b8_ffd0_4588_a581_a9413fe49cac"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">h</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The hour (common symbol: h or hr) is a unit of measurement of time. In modern usage, an hour comprises 60 minutes, or 3,600 seconds. It is approximately 1/24 of a mean solar day. An hour in the Universal Coordinated Time (UTC) time standard can include a negative or positive leap second, and may therefore have a duration of 3,599 or 3,601 seconds for adjustment purposes. Although it is not a standard defined by the International System of Units, the hour is a unit accepted for use with SI, represented by the symbol h.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Measure of time defined as 3600 seconds.</rdfs:comment>
    <emmo:EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.H02866</emmo:EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2>
    <emmo:EMMO_70fe84ff_99b6_4206_a9fc_9a8931836d84 xml:lang="en">Measure of time defined as 3600 seconds.</emmo:EMMO_70fe84ff_99b6_4206_a9fc_9a8931836d84>
    <schema:unitCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">HUR</schema:unitCode>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/siacceptedspecialunits#"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Hour</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Hour</rdfs:label>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/HR</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">The hour (common symbol: h or hr) is a unit of measurement of time. In modern usage, an hour comprises 60 minutes, or 3,600 seconds. It is approximately 1/24 of a mean solar day. An hour in the Universal Coordinated Time (UTC) time standard can include a negative or positive leap second, and may therefore have a duration of 3,599 or 3,601 seconds for adjustment purposes. Although it is not a standard defined by the International System of Units, the hour is a unit accepted for use with SI, represented by the symbol h.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#ShortTon">
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/unclassifiedunits#"/>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb3171"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb3170"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_15344998_4490_4b40_b242_c3d92e5cdc27"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_77e9dc31_5b19_463e_b000_44c6e79f98aa"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/TON_SHORT</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">The short ton is a unit of mass equal to 2,000 pounds (907.18474 kg). In the United States it is often called simply ton without distinguishing it from the metric ton (tonne, 1,000 kilograms / 2,204.62262 pounds) or the long ton (2,240 pounds / 1,016.0469088 kilograms); rather, the other two are specifically noted. There are, however, some U.S. applications for which unspecified tons normally means long tons.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Short_ton</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ShortTon</rdfs:label>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Short Ton</skos:altLabel>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The short ton is a unit of mass equal to 2,000 pounds (907.18474 kg). In the United States it is often called simply ton without distinguishing it from the metric ton (tonne, 1,000 kilograms / 2,204.62262 pounds) or the long ton (2,240 pounds / 1,016.0469088 kilograms); rather, the other two are specifically noted. There are, however, some U.S. applications for which unspecified tons normally means long tons.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ton{short}</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">ShortTon</skos:prefLabel>
