    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1058"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">A fathom = 1.8288 meters, is a unit of length in the imperial and the U.S. customary systems, used especially for measuring the depth of water. There are two yards in an imperial or U.S. fathom. Originally based on the distance between the man's outstretched arms, the size of a fathom has varied slightly depending on whether it was defined as a thousandth of an (Admiralty) nautical mile or as a multiple of the imperial yard.  Abbreviations: f, fath, fm, fth, fthm.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">fathom</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Fathom</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A fathom = 1.8288 meters, is a unit of length in the imperial and the U.S. customary systems, used especially for measuring the depth of water. There are two yards in an imperial or U.S. fathom. Originally based on the distance between the man's outstretched arms, the size of a fathom has varied slightly depending on whether it was defined as a thousandth of an (Admiralty) nautical mile or as a multiple of the imperial yard.  Abbreviations: f, fath, fm, fth, fthm.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
