    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Ångström</skos:prefLabel>
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angstrom</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">The Angstr`o`m is an internationally recognized unit of length equal to 0.1  nanometre or 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ metres.  Although accepted for use, it is not formally defined within the International System of Units(SI). The angstrom is often used in the natural sciences to express the sizes of atoms, lengths of chemical bonds and the wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation, and in technology for the dimensions of parts of integrated circuits. It is also commonly used in structural biology.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/ANGSTROM</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
