  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#InverseSquarePlanckMass">
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/PER-PlanckMass2</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Inverse Square Planck Mass</skos:altLabel>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1351"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_15344998_4490_4b40_b242_c3d92e5cdc27"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1352"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_6aa04359_50d6_43d7_b3a7_296bd391bf7d"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">In physics, the Planck mass, denoted by m_P, is the unit of mass in the system of natural units known as Planck units. It is defined so that ≈  1.2209 × 10 GeV/c₀ = 2.17651(13) × 10 kg, (or 21.7651 μ g), where c₀ is the speed of light in a vacuum, G is the gravitational constant, and ℏ is the reduced Planck constant. Particle physicists and cosmologists often use the reduced Planck mass, which is ≈ 4.341 × 10 kg = 2.435 × 10 GeV/c. The added factor of 1/√(8π) simplifies a number of equations in general relativity. Quantum effects are typified by the magnitude of Planck's constant.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planck_units</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/otherunits#"/>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">InverseSquarePlanckMass</rdfs:label>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">In physics, the Planck mass, denoted by m_P, is the unit of mass in the system of natural units known as Planck units. It is defined so that ≈  1.2209 × 10 GeV/c₀ = 2.17651(13) × 10 kg, (or 21.7651 μ g), where c₀ is the speed of light in a vacuum, G is the gravitational constant, and ℏ is the reduced Planck constant. Particle physicists and cosmologists often use the reduced Planck mass, which is ≈ 4.341 × 10 kg = 2.435 × 10 GeV/c. The added factor of 1/√(8π) simplifies a number of equations in general relativity. Quantum effects are typified by the magnitude of Planck's constant.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">InverseSquarePlanckMass</skos:prefLabel>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">/mₚ²</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
  </rdf:Description>
