    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0266ec8c_bd13_4c9c_be77_607498793ed3"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.M03980</emmo:EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2>
    <emmo:EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://dbpedia.org/resource/Mole_%28unit%29</emmo:EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25>
    <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">mol</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
    <schema:unitCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">C34</schema:unitCode>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Mole</rdfs:label>
    <emmo:EMMO_70fe84ff_99b6_4206_a9fc_9a8931836d84 xml:lang="en">The mole, symbol mol, is the SI unit of amount of substance. One mole contains exactly 6.022 140 76 × 1023 elementary entities. This number is the fixed numerical value of the Avogadro constant, NA, when expressed in the unit mol−1 and is called the Avogadro number. The amount of substance, symbol n, of a system is a measure of the number of specified elementary entities. An elementary entity may be an atom, a molecule, an ion, an electron, any other particle or specified group of particles.</emmo:EMMO_70fe84ff_99b6_4206_a9fc_9a8931836d84>
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mole_(unit)</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Mole</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/siunits#"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/MOL</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The mole, symbol mol, is the SI unit of amount of substance. One mole contains exactly 6.022 140 76 × 1023 elementary entities. This number is the fixed numerical value of the Avogadro constant, NA, when expressed in the unit mol−1 and is called the Avogadro number. The amount of substance, symbol n, of a system is a measure of the number of specified elementary entities. An elementary entity may be an atom, a molecule, an ion, an electron, any other particle or specified group of particles.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
