  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#KiloByte">
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1451"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_15d62b55_38ea_4aec_b7c4_25db1a2e5a01"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_74931b1b_c133_4e59_9a75_1bf0e1626201"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1450"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd6cec6f81ae84ad2953881773eab9f5eb1452"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8246541a_f1f6_4d03_8bd7_fc6b76d17375"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/KiloBYTE</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">The kilobyte is a multiple of the unit byte for digital information equivalent to 1000 bytes. Although the prefix kilo- means 1000, the term kilobyte and symbol kB have historically been used to refer to either 1024 (210) bytes or 1000 (103) bytes, dependent upon context, in the fields of computer science and information technology. This ambiguity is removed in QUDT, with KibiBYTE used to refer to 1024 bytes.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">kBy</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Kilo Byte</skos:altLabel>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">KiloByte</rdfs:label>
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byte?oldid=493588918</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">kB</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The kilobyte is a multiple of the unit byte for digital information equivalent to 1000 bytes. Although the prefix kilo- means 1000, the term kilobyte and symbol kB have historically been used to refer to either 1024 (210) bytes or 1000 (103) bytes, dependent upon context, in the fields of computer science and information technology. This ambiguity is removed in QUDT, with KibiBYTE used to refer to 1024 bytes.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">KiloByte</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/otherunits#"/>
  </rdf:Description>
