  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/battgpt/kg#CrystalStructure">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CrystalStructure</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nebf2c24b56c54f78a91163c4577b18e4b6"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nebf2c24b56c54f78a91163c4577b18e4b7"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The periodic crystalline arrangement of a material — its unit cell, symmetry, and atomic sites — construed as a structural aspect of the substance itself rather than as a document or description about it. This reading is what the formal axioms assert (v0.3.2 wording clarification, replacing the earlier phrase 'structural representation' which read as a semiotic artefact): hasStructure is a subproperty of emmo:hasPart, itself a subproperty of emmo:overlaps, so a material and its CrystalStructure are entailed to share parts — coherent for a physical arrangement, incoherent for a description of one. The serialised description of this structure is carried separately as a literal by hasCif. Explicitly anchored under owl:Thing (v0.3.0) for consistency with its sibling local classes (Site, UnitCell, Species, CrystalBond); no single class in EMMO core, EMMO domain-battery, EMMO domain-electrochemistry, or EMMO domain-chemical-substance cleanly captures 'the crystallographic structure of a substance', and EMMO's own domain-crystallography ontology remains an explicitly work-in-progress v0.1.0 draft not imported here, so this is left as a standalone local root rather than force-fit under an unstable upstream branch. Requires (v0.3.1) at least one hasUnitCell and one hasSpaceGroup value, matching what RDFValidator already enforced at the data level.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/battgpt/kg#"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CrystalStructure</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
  </rdf:Description>
