    <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/PlanckLength</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PlanckLength</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/unclassifiedunits#"/>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">In physics, the Planck length, denoted ℓₚ, is a unit of length, equal to 1.616199(97)×10⁻³⁵ metres. It is a base unit in the system of Planck units. The Planck length can be defined from three fundamental physical constants: the speed of light in a vacuum, Planck's constant, and the gravitational constant.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">In physics, the Planck length, denoted ℓₚ, is a unit of length, equal to 1.616199(97)×10⁻³⁵ metres. It is a base unit in the system of Planck units. The Planck length can be defined from three fundamental physical constants: the speed of light in a vacuum, Planck's constant, and the gravitational constant.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Planck Length</skos:altLabel>
    <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planck_units</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
