    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">NonEncodedContrast</rdfs:label>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">NonEncodedContrast</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_1e877c70_3b01_45a8_a8f6_8ce4f6a24660"/>
    <emmo:EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f xml:lang="en">This is a really broad class that gathers all physical phenomena in which a contrast occurs naturally, such as cloud patterns, tree rings, stains.
It doesn't mean that such contrasts cannot be used to deduce something: in fact they can be seen as indexes (in semiotic sense) of a causally connected phenomena. Simply, there is no agent behind that contrast with the intention to transmit data.</emmo:EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f>
    <emmo:EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f xml:lang="en">A non encoded contrast (e.g. a temperature variation) can be represented by data that describes (e.g. the string "today it's warmer than yesterday") or quantifies (e.g. Monday 28 °C, Tuesday 30 °C) it through a semiotic process.</emmo:EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A cloud in the sky. The radiative spectrum of a star.</skos:example>
    <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">Contrast that occurs naturally without an encoding agent producing it.</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/perspectives/contrast#"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">EnvironmentalData</skos:altLabel>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Contrast that occurs naturally without an encoding agent producing it.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
